    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.bufremove\n\n### Description\n\nWhen using `require(\"mini.bufremove\").delete()` with the last buffer in a vim tab there is some undesired behavior which causes buffers from other tabs to be assigned to the current tab. This can be very confusing and disorienting.\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.9.1\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `nvim -nu minimal.lua` (see below)\r\n2. open a file with any method, ex. `:e file_name`\r\n3. open a new tab `:tabnew`\r\n4. run `require(\"mini.bufremove\").delete()`\r\n5. See that a buffer from another tab will be moved into the tab rather than closing it.\r\n\r\n`minimal.lua`\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal lazypath = vim.fn.stdpath(\"data\") .. \"/lazy/lazy.nvim\"\r\nif not vim.loop.fs_stat(lazypath) then\r\n\tvim.fn.system({\r\n\t\t\"git\",\r\n\t\t\"clone\",\r\n\t\t\"--filter=blob:none\",\r\n\t\t\"--branch=stable\",\r\n\t\t\"https://github.com/folke/lazy.nvim.git\",\r\n\t\tlazypath,\r\n\t})\r\nend\r\nvim.opt.rtp:prepend(lazypath)\r\n\r\nrequire(\"lazy\").setup({\r\n\t\"echasnovski/mini.bufremove\",\r\n})\r\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe expected behavior should be similar to `:bdelete` in this case in which it would delete the tab if there are no more buffers to be displayed\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nAnother buffer from another tab is assigned to the window.",
