        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "🤔 hm, a vim tab is a collection of buffers managed by the user. When removing the last buffer in a vim tab it breaks down this core vim system when relocating buffers between tabs for the user. If the user has meticulously organized their tabs to hold a specific collection of buffers then this will effectively destroy this organization. Then deleting that buffer in the new tab or even just closing that tab will then remove that buffer from the other tab that it was assigned to.\r\n\r\nI do understand your reasoning though. I am already wrapping this functionality to add support for confirmation dialog so I can just add a hacky fix for this to check if it's the last buffer in a tab and just run `:bdelete` to get the appropriate core vim behavior.\r\n\r\nThanks for your time!",
